course: dictionary
lesson: 12328
title: Counterbalance: making things even
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A counterbalance is something that balances another thing by pushing or pulling the other way. A see-saw is balanced when both sides have equal weight. If one side is heavier, the see-saw tips until something on the lighter side counterbalances it.
explain: «The big weight at the back of the crane is a counterbalance, so the crane does not tip over.» The weight at the back is pushing against the heavy load at the front. Without it, the crane would fall forward.
explain: Counterbalance can also be a verb. «The sunny weather counterbalanced the cold wind.» The sun had the opposite effect to the wind, so it made up for the cold. Ask yourself: what is the opposite of this, and does it make things even?
fact: A counterbalance is a weight that balances another weight.
fact: Counterbalance can also mean something with an equal and opposite effect.
fact: «Counterbalance» as a verb means to balance something by putting weight or force on the other side.
fact: «Counterbalanced» means made up for, with an opposite effect.
fact: A crane uses a counterweight, or counterbalance, so it does not tip over when it lifts a heavy load.
exercise: «The big weight at the back of the crane is a counterbalance, so the crane does not tip over.» What is a counterbalance here? | *A weight that balances another weight | A wheel that makes the crane move | A rope that lifts the load | A colour painted on the crane
exercise: «The sunny weather counterbalanced the cold wind.» What does counterbalanced mean here? | Hid the sun behind clouds | Blew the wind away | Made the wind colder | *Made up for, with an opposite effect
exercise: Mia says, «Fun counterbalances hard work.» What does she mean? | Fun stops work from happening | Fun makes the hard work harder | Fun is the same as hard work | *Fun makes up for the hard work
test: «A bag of sand will counterbalance the box on the other end of the see-saw.» What does counterbalance mean here? | To paint the box a new colour | *To balance by putting weight on the other side | To carry the box away | To drop the box on the ground
test: Which sentence uses counterbalance as a noun, meaning a thing? | The children counterbalanced the see-saw. | The crane counterbalanced the box. | *The rock on the boat is a counterbalance. | The crane will counterbalance the load.
exam: Why does the crane have a counterbalance? | So it can fly through the air | So it can be painted a new colour | *So it does not tip over | So the load is heavier to lift
